  "worked_solution": "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $5\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n5\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{10},\\frac{\\pi }{10}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{10}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226336/assets/caacb78b0f37dcc58d6568a571c99bc569c7eb95aeacf724d5df33e35ba21fa7.png)\n\nSince there are $5$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =5\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{(\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /10}^{\\pi /10} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
